    "# MIDAA model pipeline\n",
    "This notebook provides the code to run MIDAA as supervised or unsupervised, and for normalised or raw gene expression. \n",
    "The notebook has been developed with  using https://sottorivalab.github.io/midaa/scMulti_multimodal.html for instruction on MIDAA application, and LLMs to aid code writing and concept explanations where required\n",
    "\n",
    "You should run this model on a midaa venv (See ReadMe).\n",
    "The model accepts adata and outputs matrices and information which can be processed for downstream analysis via the appropriate pipeline provided in the file (EDIT: FILENAME)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "36375d29",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#importing relevant packages\n",
    "# import midaa as maa\n",
    "import torch\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import scanpy as sc\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from scipy.sparse import issparse\n",
    "import os\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n",
    "\n",
    "import pickle \n",
    "from datetime import datetime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7a188d79",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "MIDAA is compatable with GPUs, therefore if you have access to GPUs, confirm your connection to them using this code. This code is fine to run even if you dont have GPU access, it will just output \"cpu\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3fb06bf7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Set device\n",
    "device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
    "print(f'Using device: {device}')\n",
    "if device.type == 'cuda':\n",
    "    torch.set_default_dtype(torch.float32)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5e23e94a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Preparing data for input into the model \n",
    "This code converts adata input into the required variables for the MIDAA model. \n",
    "Key considerations :\n",
    "- Depending on if your data is raw vs normalised, change maa.get_input_params_adata(adata, normalised = True or False)\n",
    "    - Check this identifies the correct input type of your data \n",
    "\n",
    "- If you want supervision in your model, uncomment the blocks for one-hot encoding and the \"side_matrices\" variuable for running the model "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "16a15777",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# loading Anndata and QCing its shape \n",
    "# adata_path = r\"FILENAME" #update if needed\n",
    "assert os.path.exists(adata_path), f'File not found: {adata_path}'\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "adata = sc.read_h5ad(adata_path)\n",
    "print(f'Loaded AnnData with shape: {adata.shape}') #compare to what you expect for QC of data input"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3db2c2e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### To add supervision:\n",
    "Convert the adata.obs column into binary labels, and convert them into a \"side matrix\" format per cell for input into the MIDAA model. Ensure you identify the type of side matrix. The choices here are G for Gaussian, P for Poisson, NB for Negative Binomial, B for Bernoulli, Beta for Beta distribution and C for Categorical."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f9423ea5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "#using sklearn package to aid binarisation - code aided by Cursor AI \n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelBinarizer\n",
    "\n",
    "treatment_labels = adata.obs['treatment'].astype(str).values #converting to string to work with lb\n",
    "lb = LabelBinarizer()\n",
    "\n",
    "y_onehot = lb.fit_transform(treatment_labels) #learns and transforms each label \n",
    "if y_onehot.shape[1] == 1:  # binary case - returns a single column of 0 and 1 if there are only 2 classes \n",
    "    y_onehot = np.hstack([1 - y_onehot, y_onehot])\n",
    "\n",
    "side_matrices = [y_onehot.astype(np.float32)] #allocating \"side_matrices\" variable for MIDAA model input\n",
    "input_types_side = [\"C\"] #setting as categorical loss for the treamtent labels, see above for options\n",
    "\n",
    "#QC prints \n",
    "print(f'Classes: {lb.classes_}') #should print what you expect to supervise by #QC on side matrix \n",
    "print(\"Example side_matrices (first 5 rows):\")\n",
    "\n",
    "#checking the labels should match the obs\n",
    "print(side_matrices[0][:5])\n",
    "print (adata.obs['treatment'].head())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "27761e45",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "MIDAA provides a helpful function to go from adata to the input variables the model needs (data, normalisation, and distribution). \n",
    "\n",
    "Please take care in setting adata_is_normalized = False if the data is raw, otherwise if the data is normalised the default function is appropriate. Check the printed output for input_distribution type - for raw it should be NB, for normalised it should be G\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "In the case that your adata is scipy sparse for memory efficiency, it will need to be converted to a MIDAA compliant torche tensor - please use the bottom code. If your adata is not sparse, no need. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b785bf92",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# #non-sparse scipy adata \n",
    "# input_data, normalization, input_distribution = maa.get_input_params_adata(adata) #default assumes normalised datas, if raw set adata_is_normalized = False\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#Sparse scipy data \n",
    "input_data_raw, normalization_raw, input_distribution = maa.get_input_params_adata(adata) #default assumes normalised datas, if raw set, add variable in function for adata_is_normalized = False\n",
    "\n",
    "#converting to compatable form, aided by ChatGPT4 - not expected to impact the data values themselves just its format compatability\n",
    "def scipy_to_torch_sparse(csr_mat, device):\n",
    "    coo = csr_mat.tocoo()\n",
    "    indices = torch.tensor(np.vstack((coo.row, coo.col)), dtype=torch.long, device=device)\n",
    "    values = torch.tensor(coo.data, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\n",
    "    shape = coo.shape\n",
    "    return torch.sparse_coo_tensor(indices, values, shape).coalesce()\n",
    "\n",
    "def convert_input_list(data_list):\n",
    "    torch_list = []\n",
    "    for x in data_list:\n",
    "        if issparse(x):\n",
    "            torch_list.append(scipy_to_torch_sparse(x, device))\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            if isinstance(x, torch.Tensor):\n",
    "                torch_list.append(x.to(device=device, dtype=torch.float32))\n",
    "            else:\n",
    "                torch_list.append(torch.tensor(x, dtype=torch.float32, device=device))\n",
    "    return torch_list\n",
    "\n",
    "input_data = convert_input_list(input_data_raw)\n",
    "normalization = convert_input_list(normalization_raw)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#Check the\n",
    "print (input_distribution) #QC should be G for normalised counts and NB for raw counts"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c2216fc7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Running the MIDAA model \n",
    "MIDAA has some hyperparameter which can be tuned, but I would suggest using the ones I have deualt set here and playing about from the plots that get given. Primary tuning should be done from learning rate (as per MIDAA author feedback) and netowrk size (as per my experiments).\n",
    "\n",
    "A key input is the archetype_range. MIDAA reruns the model for every numbr of archetypes and you can identify the best performing one by tradeing off ELBO value with interpretability by identifying the the elbow in the ELBO plot created. 2-12 is a reasonable range to go for, as more than 11 archetypes would make the resulting archetypes difficult to interpret\n",
    "\n",
    "Key considerations here:\n",
    "- if you are using a supervised model, make sure you uncomment the side_matrices and just_VAE inputs\n",
    "- The training process can take a while, so I have input alot of save functions so you can access every trained archetype model as the model is training. If you need to understand how the ELBO plot is looking before the full range of archetypes have been completed, please see (EDIT: ) for pipeline to plot the elbos of the models loaded already. Please change the file names to yours!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "eb283efc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Hyperparameters for tuning \n",
    "MULT = 4  # Network width multiplier to easily scale \n",
    "archetype_range = range(2, 12)  # change range as needed \n",
    "steps = 2000  # training steps \n",
    "lr = 5e-4  #  learning rate\n",
    "batch_size = 4096  # batch size\n",
    "torch_seed = 42  # for reproducibility\n",
    "results = {} #intialise results dictionary\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "dd55caf6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#Running the modek MIDAA for a range of archetypes\n",
    "for narchetypes in archetype_range:\n",
    "    print([x.device for x in input_data])\n",
    "    print([x.device for x in normalization])\n",
    "    print(f'\\n Training with {narchetypes} archetypes')\n",
    "    result = maa.fit_MIDAA(\n",
    "        input_data,\n",
    "        normalization,\n",
    "        input_distribution,\n",
    "        side_matrices=side_matrices, #newinput for classification loss\n",
    "        input_types_side=input_types_side,\n",
    "        hidden_dims_dec_common=[64 * MULT, 128 * MULT],\n",
    "        hidden_dims_dec_last=[256 * MULT],\n",
    "        hidden_dims_enc_ind=[128 * MULT],\n",
    "        hidden_dims_enc_common=[64 * MULT],\n",
    "        hidden_dims_enc_pre_Z=[32 * MULT],\n",
    "        lr=lr,\n",
    "        gamma_lr=0.1,\n",
    "        steps=steps,\n",
    "        narchetypes=narchetypes,\n",
    "        batch_size=batch_size,\n",
    "        torch_seed=torch_seed,\n",
    "        CUDA=(device.type == 'cuda'),\n",
    "        just_VAE=False #ensures classification loss is included\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    results[narchetypes] = result  # Save the full result dict\n",
    "    print(f'Final ELBO for {narchetypes} archetypes: {result[\"ELBO\"][-1]:.2f}')\n",
    "\n",
    "    #_____________________SAVING THE MODEL RESULTS_________________________\n",
    "    # Create a clean dictionary with only picklable data that has been created by the MIDAA model\n",
    "    saveable_data = { \n",
    "        \"inferred_quantities\": {}, #will provide the matrices and other interesting quantities of the trained model\n",
    "        \"ELBO\": result[\"ELBO\"], #provides the ELBO values over training\n",
    "        \"hyperparameters\": result[\"hyperparameters\"] #provides the hyperparameters used in the model for recreation\n",
    "    }\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Extract and convert tensors to numpy arrays for the inferred quantities (aided by Cursor AI to help with torch tensor compatability)\n",
    "    for key, value in result[\"inferred_quantities\"].items():\n",
    "        if isinstance(value, torch.Tensor):\n",
    "            saveable_data[\"inferred_quantities\"][key] = value.detach().cpu().numpy()\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            saveable_data[\"inferred_quantities\"][key] = value\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    # I suggest putting your files in a specified directory as theyre generated \n",
    "    output_dir = \"EDIT\" #change this to your preferred directory\n",
    "    os.makedirs(output_dir, exist_ok=True) #Create output directory if it doesn't exist\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Use filepath to save files with the results and the model path\n",
    "    result_filename = os.path.join(output_dir, f\"Name_{narchetypes}arch.pkl\") #update this as you need\n",
    "    with open(result_filename, \"wb\") as f:\n",
    "        pickle.dump(saveable_data, f)\n",
    "    print(f\"Successfully saved clean result dictionary to {result_filename}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Save the model state dict (model weights)\n",
    "    model_filename = os.path.join(output_dir, f\"Name_{narchetypes}arch.pth\") #update this as you need\n",
    "    if \"deepAA_obj\" in result: #model object output of MIDAA\n",
    "        torch.save(result[\"deepAA_obj\"].state_dict(), model_filename)\n",
    "        print(f\"Successfully saved model weights to {model_filename}\")\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        print(\"Warning: No 'deepAA_obj' found in result\") #QC check \n",
    "\n",
    "    # For supervised models only: Save the side matrix / classification-related information if present\n",
    "    # This assumes the decoder returns (input_reconstructed, side_reconstructed)\n",
    "    # if \"deepAA_obj\" in best_result and \"inferred_quantities\" in best_result:\n",
    "    #     deepAA_obj = best_result[\"deepAA_obj\"]\n",
    "    #     Z = best_result[\"inferred_quantities\"].get(\"Z\", None)\n",
